  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 AM CDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nThe upper air pattern early this morning depicts a developing low \noff the Pacific Northwest coast with a couple of weaker shortwaves \nwithin broad southwest flow from the Desert Southwest through the \nPlains region. Scattered high cloud cover is increasing across the \nstate as a result of this shortwave activity. There will probably be \nlittle impact from these waves in our area aside from these clouds, \nthough it is worth noting CAMs are showing brief, isolated showers \nin north central KS this morning. Forecast soundings have enough mid-\nlevel dry air that it would seem hard pressed for much, if any, rain \nto reach the ground, but can't rule out a stray shower or sprinkle \nfor a short time. Temperatures should be similar to or slightly \nwarmer than yesterday with highs in the upper 60s to low 70s. \nDespite the NBM running a bit cool the last two days, opted not to \nstray from it too much for today given the increase in clouds and \ndecrease in southerly wind. It's a similar story for Tuesday both in \nterms of temperatures and a brief, low rain chance in the area with \nshortwave energy still overhead.\n\nBy Wednesday and Thursday, the Pacific trough amplifies and digs \nacross the western CONUS, helping to strengthen the ridge over the \ncentral US. This favors temperatures warming further as mid-level \nheights rise, with morning lows in the 50s and afternoon highs in \nthe mid to upper 70s (which is 10-15 degrees above average for mid-\nOctober). There is still very little spread through this time frame, \nbut curiously, highs have trended cooler Thursday and Friday despite \ngood agreement in the ridge axis still being overhead and the low-\nlevel wind field increasing with lee troughing ahead of the \napproaching system. The first lead shortwave looks to lift northeast \nFriday and keep us dry, with better rain chances coming Saturday. \nThat said, models notably diverge with the evolution of the upper \ntrough by the weekend, leading to more uncertainty with the timing \nof associated surface fronts and rain chances. As a result, PoPs \nremain below 50% through the weekend and temperature ranges widen to \n10-15 degrees by Sunday.",
